rPerfect 👍 thanks for clarifying. Since you’re running DataLens in Docker, here’s the key point:

When you put localhost inside DataLens connection settings, that means inside the container, not on your Mac.
That’s why you got the connection refused error earlier — the container couldn’t see your Mac’s PostgreSQL on localhost:5432.

✅ How to connect PostgreSQL (on your Mac) to DataLens (Docker)

You have two main options:

Option 1: Use host.docker.internal

Docker provides a special hostname that maps to your host machine (your Mac).

In DataLens connection form, set:

Host: host.docker.internal
Port: 5432
Database: postgres
User: apple  (or the role you created)
Password: (whatever you set)


Make sure PostgreSQL is listening on 0.0.0.0 or at least on 127.0.0.1.

Check in /usr/local/var/postgresql@14/postgresql.conf:

listen_addresses = '*'


And in pg_hba.conf (same folder), allow connections:

host    all    all    all    md5


Then restart Postgres:

pg_ctl -D /usr/local/var/postgresql@14 restart

Option 2: Run Postgres in Docker

Much simpler, you can run both DataLens and Postgres in the same Docker network.

Example:

docker network create datalens-net

docker run -d --name pg14 --network datalens-net \
  -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret \
  -e POSTGRES_USER=apple \
  -e POSTGRES_DB=postgres \
  -p 5432:5432 postgres:14


Then run DataLens attached to the same network:

docker run -d --name datalens --network datalens-net -p 8080:8080 your-datalens-image


In DataLens connection form, set:

Host: pg14
Port: 5432
User: apple
Password: secret
Database: postgres


⚡ Since you already have PostgreSQL running on your Mac via Homebrew, the fastest fix is Option 1 (host.docker.internal).

